{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me","id":662532,"title":"Can someone explain superhero comics to me?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-09-12T19:12:10-07:00","id":662532,"title":"Can someone explain superhero comics to me?","content":"Just right at the outset: my intent is not to bash on comics. Whenever I say something negative about them, my intent is to explain an issue (heh, pun unintended but also unregretted) with what I see as comics as a whole, with the expectation that there <i>is</i> a legitimate reason/explanation which I, in my silliness, have missed.<br /> <br /> But, I really don't understand the appeal? Not of graphic novels generally, I've read a few and thoroughly enjoyed them; they're a perfectly valid, and sometimes the best, way to tell a story. I'm talking specifically about superhero comics. From what extremely little I've read, and what slightly more I've read about, just tracing the continuity can be a pain in the neck, what with the crossovers, reboots, partial reboots, different versions of the same character run simultaneously, jumping around in time (both due to in-universe time travel and odd ordering of stories) and continuity errors. Then we have the simple problems with knowing the order; again, from what very little I've been able to see, they're organised as basically a series of mini-series, but I have yet to find any kind of logical guide to what order those mini-series come in.<br /> <br /> For example, I find the Hulk to be an interesting character, and wanted to get into him a bit more by reading his comic series. So, I understood it's been rebooted several times since the original, when the Hulk was grey and changed at night, which is fine, so I went looking for the 'current' version.<br /> <br /> Two hours, three forums, two wiki-crawls over two seperate wikis and innumeral Googlings later, I gave up. I think I figured out what was the current 'start', but then I read it, and it was referring to a whole bunch of stuff which I had no idea about, and it seemed really, really important (there seemed to be a Justice-League-equivalent involved? I know it wasn't the JL since they're DC, but same thing). I just want to read the Hulk; I don't object to reading others, but it shouldn't be required reading, to my mind. Also, money constraints: each issue/book costs $5-$20, and each other book I need to keep up with multiplies that.<br /> <br /> Again, I'm not looking to bash on comics; a lot of people read them, and they seem to enjoy them. In accordance with the principle of self-skepticism, I assume that there's a logic or fact or something I've missed. Can someone please, please explain it to me, because I just can't figure it out myself?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-12T19:54:03-07:00","id":662586,"title":"","content":"The continuity can be difficult to track in a grand scale - which is why most comic fans suggest that any new reader looking to get started either start at an issue #0 (not all series have these, they are usually a prequel, a teaser, or some other type of tale that introduces the character and the general idea of the series in a form similar to a pilot episode for a TV series) or #1, and then just stick to that comic title for a while - when a crossover comes along, it will tell you which issues of which titles are involved in the story so that you don't have to guess, and that might result in you finding that you liked a character that crossed into the title you were following and you can then keep up with issues of that from that point forward.<br /> <br /> Or, at the very least, starting at the first issue involved in a storyline - often times the issue number will be something far from #1, like #78 or even #212, but it will have a title on the cover both for the comic series itself (i.e. Uncanny X-Men) and for the storyline (i.e. Age of Apocalypse - part 1). And, again, from there following that tale just requires picking up issues in the order released or picking up the specifically mentioned issues when the storyline bleeds over to other comic series or has crossover.<br /> <br /> As for the costs, I agree that they can very quickly get quite high... that was even true back when I was buying issues at $2 each as a kid. These days I avoid the costs by waiting for compilation books that collect a whole storyline into a single, often hard-cover volume that has a lower price than buying each issue along the way would have.<br /> <br /> And really, if you stumble across a character that you identify with or that resonates with you (in my case, that's The Punisher, Carol Danvers/Captain Marvel, Doctor Strange, and some but not all author's takes on Wolverine) that is when the whole thing clicks and you realize what it is about comics that digs its hooks in so deep that even people that &quot;grew out of it&quot; can flip open a comic and find a smile on their face and what drives the astronomical sales figures on the recent barrage of super-hero movies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not so noble anymore.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1823-drake","name":"Drake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1823_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"456"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-12T20:00:37-07:00","id":662592,"title":"","content":"This is one of the reasons I never attempted to read any comics from Marvel or DC's main continuities. Marvel Mangaverse, Transformers, The Authority, Rising Stars, these are all titles I've greatly enjoyed. Except for TF, they're all Superheroes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-12T20:07:53-07:00","id":662596,"title":"","content":"Some people like the trivia and 'expanded universe.' Just like some folks like to pore over Star Trek or Wars or Game of Thrones, cartoons or anime or even World of Darkness, seeking out details and developing theories from the books and scenes, some of those people turn their minds and interest to comics.<br /> <br /> Others ignore the extra details. For instance if I were you I'd probably find a noteworthy issue of the Hulk from around 2000 or 1990 if I was feeling bold and just start there, reading forward. If you're not interested in the Defenders or Ultimate Hulk, just stick to the 'main' line of them. If you end up liking some other character depicted in a crossover or a team-up, you can then follow them. Joe Casey took over Hulk near 1999, I enjoy him as a writer, which leads to other things.<br /> <br /> Some people just like specific writers more than characters or teams, and will wait until they have a run on an ongoing title or do a mini or limited series.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-12-2015, 11:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-13T03:00:27-07:00","id":662860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=662596#post662596\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some people like the trivia and 'expanded universe.' Just like some folks like to pore over Star Trek or Wars or Game of Thrones, cartoons or anime or even World of Darkness, seeking out details and developing theories from the books and scenes, some of those people turn their minds and interest to comics.<br /> <br /> Others ignore the extra details. For instance if I were you I'd probably find a noteworthy issue of the Hulk from around 2000 or 1990 if I was feeling bold and just start there, reading forward. If you're not interested in the Defenders or Ultimate Hulk, just stick to the 'main' line of them. If you end up liking some other character depicted in a crossover or a team-up, you can then follow them. Joe Casey took over Hulk near 1999, I enjoy him as a writer, which leads to other things.</div> </div> </div> </div>I figured it was a preference thing, I'm just trying to figure out how it's kept all straight. You say &quot;pick a noteworthy issue and read forward&quot;. Fair enough advice: what's a noteworthy issue? How do I figure out which comes after what? Given that the <i>Incredible</i> <i>Hulk</i> series seems to randomly end in 2000, what came after? What do I pick up: Planet Hulk? World War Hulk? Uncanny Origins? Marvel Age Hulk?<br /> <br /> Hopefully you begin to grasp my no-doubt-clumsily-explained point. There's just so much, and the order/pattern seems completely unintuitive. I took another look at the Hulk character, and found a reference, at last, to the title being changed from Incredible Hulk to just Hulk in 1999, but then apparently changed back in 2000, which then changed to Incredible Hercules (?), but the Hulk himself continued on in Hulk volume 2, before changing <i>back</i> to Incredible Hulk again in 2009, and then soon after into Incredible Hulk<i>s</i>, then <i>back again</i> to Incredible Hulk volume 4, then The Indestructible Hulk, and then I stopped checking because, really, <i>seriously</i>?<br /> <br /> And I have no idea if there were spin-offs in there or what. Thankfully, I found a kind of coda for what is continuity for each of the above runs, so that'd be solved, but jeez louise. I don't mind stories being complex, I don't mind stories having reference to other stories or characters or crossovers. That's fine and can be a lot of fun. I can't help but think this level of homework just to find out what the current series is called seems a mite extreme.<br /> <br /> So does each issue have like a little thing on the inside of the cover or something telling you &quot;this follows X, and is followed by Y, and you need to have read Z, A, B and C&quot;? Is that how it works? Or do people just rely on collections nowadays? Because I can certainly see the appeal of those, but then we sort-of have the same problem of knowing which collection came when, and follows what continuity...<br /> <br /> It has to be easier than it seems. No media could survive being that complex just to work out what comes after what, so I'm clearly missing something. (Assuming this pattern is representative across characters, of course, which it seems to be.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":207,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","user":"Allan53","date":". 09-13-2015, 06:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-13T06:50:06-07:00","id":663039,"title":"","content":"The Hulk probably isn't the best example. But most of the &quot;big name A list&quot; heroes suffer from this; especially ones that have seen rather massive up and down swings in popularity. While you say no media could survive this sort of thing... the big name comics actually thrive on it. Their core customer base - especially in the digital age - are hardcore readers. The more popular the character is, the more you can expect an increase in difficulty tracking their continuity. The hardcore fan base tends to buy into it for greater returns than an potential lost casual reader.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-13T13:18:31-07:00","id":663423,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=662860#post662860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I figured it was a preference thing, I'm just trying to figure out how it's kept all straight. You say &quot;pick a noteworthy issue and read forward&quot;. Fair enough advice: what's a noteworthy issue? How do I figure out which comes after what? Given that the <i>Incredible</i> <i>Hulk</i> series seems to randomly end in 2000, what came after? What do I pick up: Planet Hulk? World War Hulk? Uncanny Origins? Marvel Age Hulk?</div> </div> </div> </div>What's noteworthy would be something for you to decide, something you like might be more accurate. Moving on from there works numerically. The Incredible Hulk was sort of 1999, as you pointed out. The Incredible Hulk, volume 1, ended with issue #474. Then began the Hulk series, which changed its name to the Incredible Hulk, volume 2, at issue 12. Planet Hulk and World War Hulk are story arcs within the Incredible Hulk, volume 2, line. So if you started with the Hulk series you'd eventually get there. If you were just interested in the story arc, there's probably some compilations in the form of trade paperbacks, which would probably be best if you wanted to read all the tie-ins, as those stories had crossovers with Ghost Rider and Amazing Fantasy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hopefully you begin to grasp my no-doubt-clumsily-explained point. There's just so much, and the order/pattern seems completely unintuitive. I took another look at the Hulk character, and found a reference, at last, to the title being changed from Incredible Hulk to just Hulk in 1999, but then apparently changed back in 2000, which then changed to Incredible Hercules (?), but the Hulk himself continued on in Hulk volume 2, before changing <i>back</i> to Incredible Hulk again in 2009, and then soon after into Incredible Hulk<i>s</i>, then <i>back again</i> to Incredible Hulk volume 4, then The Indestructible Hulk, and then I stopped checking because, really, <i>seriously</i>? </div> </div> </div>This is what I get for reading slowly. Yeah with the first part you have the reboot I was mentioning and the retitling is just that, a retitling. All the comics have numbers and dates on them, which should let you track chronological order.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I have no idea if there were spin-offs in there or what. Thankfully, I found a kind of coda for what is continuity for each of the above runs, so that'd be solved, but jeez louise. I don't mind stories being complex, I don't mind stories having reference to other stories or characters or crossovers. That's fine and can be a lot of fun. I can't help but think this level of homework just to find out what the current series is called seems a mite extreme. </div> </div> </div>There's tons of spin-offs and crossovers and limited runs scattered around there. It's sort of the benefit to collecting trades, they tend to compile everything in one simple book. There are some people who have seen your problem and attempted to give advice, like <a href=\"http://hulkepicness.tumblr.com/howtoread\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> and <a href=\"http://www.tradereadingorder.com/marvel-character/hulk/page/2/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. At least in regards to reading past comic books. The whole issue of it being hard to get into has actually caused some, I guess, problems in the publishing industry. It's why they have line and universe-wide reboots in the first place, giving people a #1 to jump into. As you noted there was one around 2000, and when it turned into Incredible Hulk another. I liked the Indestructible Hulk line, myself, not normally that much of a fan of the Hulk I thought it had Banner sort of doing a more responsible thing. There is another coming up soon, with Amadeus Cho as the new Hulk.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So does each issue have like a little thing on the inside of the cover or something telling you &quot;this follows X, and is followed by Y, and you need to have read Z, A, B and C&quot;? Is that how it works? Or do people just rely on collections nowadays? Because I can certainly see the appeal of those, but then we sort-of have the same problem of knowing which collection came when, and follows what continuity... </div> </div> </div>Generally they just expect you to go numerically. Incredible Hulk v1 1-474, Hulk 1-11, Incredible Hulk v2 12-111, then either Incredible Hercules 112-141 if your following the comic, or Hulk v2, for that Red Hulk goodness. Hulk turns into Red-She Hulk, and the Indestructible Hulk line pops up parallel to it near the end. I fully appreciate how confusing it is, there's a hard balance between just sticking with the numbers or doing reboots and retitlings in order to lure new readers in, I'm just trying to explain. If there is a reboot or a retitling or the line is ending but the character is moving to another comic, like a team one, it will say so usually at the end of the story, sometimes the writers will give more detail or it will say where you can 'Look for more of...' a character in some other comic. If there's a big crossover, there's often a 'Reading List' page with all the involved comics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It has to be easier than it seems. No media could survive being that complex just to work out what comes after what, so I'm clearly missing something. (Assuming this pattern is representative across characters, of course, which it seems to be.) </div> </div> </div>Two things make it incredibly easy. One, following now. It's easy to look at decades of writing and changes and see it as a big ugly mess but if you were following it at the time it would be easier. Obviously this doesn't help you get into the already published issues but if you wait a few months, as currently Marvel is doing a huge sweeping crossover to lead up to an across-the-line change, you'll get the brand new, and I kid you not, <a href=\"http://www.comicvine.com/articles/the-identity-of-totally-awesome-hulk-revealed/1100-153464/\" target=\"_blank\">'The Totally Awesome Hulk.'</a> And from there it's just a matter of getting a new issue each month. The second way is the trade paperbacks. They tend to be organized better, ideally collecting the 'best' story arcs and including spinoffs and side issues or crossovers. It's going to be a hell of a lot easier to get the TPBs than it would be to track down the individual comics offline.<br /> <br /> As Heavy Arms pointed out the Hulk has sort of a weird history, making it especially difficult with him. Something other comics are easier, with reboots rare or nonexistent. Invincible, for instance, has the benefit of being mostly self-contained.<br /> <br /> Oh also both comic companies have wikia which help organize things. <a href=\"http://marvel.wikia.com/wiki/Hulk_Vol_1\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a> is the page for Hulk volume 1, clicking on individual comics will show you a brief outline or characters involved, the previous and next comics even if the title has changed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-13-2015, 04:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-14T01:39:28-07:00","id":664047,"title":"","content":"Here is my advice: don't worry too much about older continuity. If you want to read a major story arc for a given hero or team, Marvel publishes trades with the banner &quot;Essential&quot; for all their big name characters, that compile important story lines; ask for them at your Local Comic Store(LCS). You can also ask your LCS for Marvel &quot;Visionaries&quot; titles if you want to follow a specific writer's work on a title. I highly recommend Peter David's legendary 10 year run on Hulk.<br /> <br /> Planet Hulk happens before World War Hulk, and events in PH are the reason for WWH.<br /> <br /> Titles change or start over at #1 as a marketing ploy, #1s and anniversary issues like #50, #100, or number #300, sell very well. This is also why you see stuff like &quot;Incredible Herc&quot;, it's just a gimmick to get people who were already reading Hulk's book to try Herc's book.<br /> <br /> Oh, and don't get too attached to adjectives, like &quot;Incredible&quot;; most of the time, these words don't really change anything, they're just there to grab attention, or differentiate one volume of a hero's story from another. The one exception in Marvel is &quot;Ultimate&quot;, most Marvel &quot;Ultimate&quot; stories happen in a completely different timeline than the main, or &quot;616&quot; universe. The only exception to THAT rule is current issues of &quot;Ultimate Spider-Man&quot;, which will be taking place in 616, but feature the Spider-Man from Ultimate, as the Ultimate universe has been destroyed.<br /> <br /> If you feel you need to know the origin story of a character, there is also a reasonably good wiki-type database at <a href=\"http://www.comicvine.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.comicvine.com</a>.<br /> <br /> Sadly, recap pages at the beginning, and preview pages at the end of comics died out some time ago, as that page count was sacrificed in the name of advertising space.<br /> <br /> I hope this helps. As for DC, just start with New52 Justice League Volume 1.<br /> DC history is so fluid, you could drink it with a straw. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/12.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div> &quot;A free society is one in which it is safe to be unpopular.&quot;<br /> <span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\">I find <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=3&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwin4NXQzLfXAhVGySYKHV3TDXYQFgg3MAI&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.ign.com%2Farticles%2F2017%2F04%2F25%2Fmarvels-nick-spencer-on-captain-americas-betrayal-and-handling-fan-reactions&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EcsX7n5ebntygb0TizyGl\" target=\"_blank\">Nick Spencer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=4&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiK7d7szLfXAhXCWSYKHTpKBbcQFgg4MAM&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRichard_B._Spencer&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tuP6phfINia-bh_jWsDT_\" target=\"_blank\">Richard Spencer</a> to be equally repulsive.</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":6138,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","user":"Mangle77","date":". 09-14-2015, 08:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","name":"Mangle77","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6138_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-14T14:08:14-07:00","id":664727,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mangle77</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=664047#post664047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">DC history is so fluid, you could drink it with a straw. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>This is sarcasm, I hope.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-14T16:57:32-07:00","id":665059,"title":"","content":"Considering how hard Superboy Prime literally punched the DC continuity.... I'm fairly sure it's safe to say it is pulpy enough to drink.<br /> <br /> The thing about the DC continuity is that DC completely embraces continuity reboots by having them actually happen in-universe. It does create some useful starting points for the different DC continuities (as noted, the New 52), though they usually have some artifacts that can be out of place if you didn't know about how the continuity reboot happened. It also creates other problems where titles that didn't really need the reboot are forced into it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-14T17:18:31-07:00","id":665070,"title":"","content":"Superboy Prime's changes were minimal, really. At least regarding bigger name characters. Jason Todd was resurrected, Donna Troy and Hawkman's multiple origins streamlined into one. Some minor changes and retcons, Geoff Johns taking the opportunity to erase Hal Jordan's arrest for drunk driving. Someone was savvy enough to make a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuity_changes_during_Infinite_Crisis#cite_note-ReferenceB-7\" target=\"_blank\">wikipedia entry</a> for the changes.<br /> <br /> The main thing is, if you want Detective Comics, you can start at 1 or 27, then go all the way up to 881, then volume 2 starts with the New 52. Action Comics are the same, Wonder Woman's headlining comic is Wonder Woman, through all variations. Justice League had some variations in names but they were usually, at least, in chronological order. JLA was probably the best run up till issue 100.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-14T17:51:39-07:00","id":665102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=665059#post665059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> It also creates other problems where titles that didn't really need the reboot are forced into it.</div> </div> </div> </div> Teen Titans springs to mind, as does pretty much any title with a teen/very early 20s protagonist at the start of New52. Cyborg and Nightwing were the only ones that didn't basically get nuked by Flashpoint. (The in story event that brought about New52)<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/12.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div> &quot;A free society is one in which it is safe to be unpopular.&quot;<br /> <span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\">I find <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=3&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwin4NXQzLfXAhVGySYKHV3TDXYQFgg3MAI&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.ign.com%2Farticles%2F2017%2F04%2F25%2Fmarvels-nick-spencer-on-captain-americas-betrayal-and-handling-fan-reactions&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EcsX7n5ebntygb0TizyGl\" target=\"_blank\">Nick Spencer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=4&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiK7d7szLfXAhXCWSYKHTpKBbcQFgg4MAM&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRichard_B._Spencer&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tuP6phfINia-bh_jWsDT_\" target=\"_blank\">Richard Spencer</a> to be equally repulsive.</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","name":"Mangle77","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6138_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T05:31:43-07:00","id":665565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mangle77</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=664047#post664047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Titles change or start over at #1 as a marketing ploy, #1s and anniversary issues like #50, #100, or number #300, sell very well. This is also why you see stuff like &quot;Incredible Herc&quot;, it's just a gimmick to get people who were already reading Hulk's book to try Herc's book.<br /> <br /> Oh, and don't get too attached to adjectives, like &quot;Incredible&quot;; most of the time, these words don't really change anything, they're just there to grab attention, or differentiate one volume of a hero's story from another. The one exception in Marvel is &quot;Ultimate&quot;, most Marvel &quot;Ultimate&quot; stories happen in a completely different timeline than the main, or &quot;616&quot; universe. The only exception to THAT rule is current issues of &quot;Ultimate Spider-Man&quot;, which will be taking place in 616, but feature the Spider-Man from Ultimate, as the Ultimate universe has been destroyed.<br /> <br /> If you feel you need to know the origin story of a character, there is also a reasonably good wiki-type database at <a href=\"http://www.comicvine.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.comicvine.com</a>.<br /> <br /> Sadly, recap pages at the beginning, and preview pages at the end of comics died out some time ago, as that page count was sacrificed in the name of advertising space.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that's depressing. When even the number changes aren't a reliable guide...<br /> <br /> Thanks for the Hulk insight, but I was sort-of leaning more towards Iron Man these days; of the Marvel movies, he was really the only one of the characters I really could get behind: Incredible Hulk was good, but Avengers made some changes that I didn't like, Thor was dull (to me), and they did the best they reasonably could have with Captain America but I just see him as ultimately kind of a flat character. Whereas Iron Man/Tony Stark is so flawed, yet so heroic, that I find him really interesting. Thankfully, IM does seem to have a comparatively simpler time drawing a line telling his stories. Not sure where it 'started' in the current run, but I'll see if I can hunt that down.<br /> <br /> Thanks for the tip, I actually found comicvine in my most recent eyeballing, but good to get reinforcement that it is a useful source.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T10:48:09-07:00","id":665889,"title":"","content":"A good place to start might be Civil War, then. If you just want Iron Man stuff you can go into Iron Man volume 2, it eventually expands into the rest of the Civil War. If you're interested in the wider scope of things, the New Avengers from 2005 is a good start. You'll want to check out the Illuminati mini, too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T14:22:46-07:00","id":666067,"title":"","content":"I found a reference saying Extremis is a good place to jump in? Or does that involve too much backstory knowledge?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T14:40:39-07:00","id":666076,"title":"","content":"Not at all, it's a good one to start with, that's the one I was suggesting though I'm not sure why I called it volume 2. It's volume 4. The series starts in 2005. Plus it's originally handled by Warren Ellis, a particularly good writer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-15-2015, 05:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T15:18:14-07:00","id":666108,"title":"","content":"Ah, right.<br /> <br /> But seriously, this could be much, much simpler to follow. I mean, it can only put people off getting in, I'd imagine?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T16:10:07-07:00","id":666121,"title":"","content":"That's why they do reboots. Everyone can start on issue 1. The problem is it alienates some of the older fans. Then sales don't do well. And they have to cancel it, to make a new comic. Then a couple years down the line they try again. But without a complete reboot they have to hope people can follow the 'new' story, or that old fans will enjoy the continuation. They don't just change the name to confuse you, it's usually because of complaints or poor sales.<br /> <br /> It's all very tricky. The big balance is between attracting new fans and keeping old ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-15-2015, 07:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T20:04:05-07:00","id":666349,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I get that. Problem is that issue 1 isn't always continuity-reset, particularly within new volumes that are the same story and involve stuff from previous volumes, so that's not a particularly useful guide either.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-15T20:57:57-07:00","id":666423,"title":"","content":"Generally an issue 1 won't continue directly from the end of a previous ongoing unless there's some sort of massive change, like the New 52 stuff or this upcoming Marvel thing. A lot of comics are going to appreciate that you know the basics, like Bruce Banner was exposed to gamma radiation and when he gets angry becomes the incredible green Hulk, so they won't always completely change a character. But there are usually some style changes and a new start. It's more of a soft reboot. Some obvious things may have changed, but it usually just won't mention them, so some later writer can pick up the slack and get some nostalgia fandom via recurring bad guys if they want, but alternatively you're not tied down to decades of previous continuity and fans worrying about continuity inconsistencies. That's what they did with Extremis, I <i>think</i>. It was like the movie, but basically appreciated that Tony Stark has been Iron Man, he's done some unspecified superheroing, otherwise its a new start. The big two did this with a lot of lines after the 90s.<br /> <br /> First issues sell, though. It's one of the few things you can really count on. If it's a big name and the first issue, it's going to sell a lot. So if they're on issue 30 of a run that sells poorly, and no one's really talking about it, some writer comes to you with a new take on the character, why not go for a reboot?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-16-2015, 12:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-17T08:11:23-07:00","id":668270,"title":"","content":"I'd like nothing more than to join in a read the Marvel stuff, but I'll stick to the movies I think. I'm one of those people who gets incredibly confused by the constant rebooting of different lines, at different times, but things all look the same (To my uneducated eyes). I also dislike the cross-over stuff; In my mind the Xmen live off in Xmen Universe and don't cross over to the Avengers or Fantastic 4. Otherwise surely Iron Man would be complaining about constant mutant riots and these massive Sentinel things etc.<br /> <br /> I did read Spawn for a while, in my youth, but lost my way with it and even that went a bit weird with different time-periods covered. I tried with Fathom, Witchblade, Darkness and again it seemed to get very convoluted quite quickly.<br /> <br /> For me the logic of jumping in on a #0 or #1 makes perfect sense ... but everything appears to reference each other? Or mention it in passing like multi-verses in some of the worlds. It all just appears needlessly and stupdily complex and confusing. I don't want to start getting into &quot;Hero #1&quot; through to issue #10 to then have it reference something two reboots ago as part of some shared continuity that I then look back at and see is some massive list of issues to read through (Purchase!) to get up to speed.<br /> <br /> At least the movies are all seperate right ... though the news of Spiderman in the Avengers confused me until I read he was always part of that team or something?<br /> <br /> I'll stick to the movies I think .... less brain-damaging!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/907-jay","name":"Jay","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar907_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-17T11:46:41-07:00","id":668543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jay</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=668270#post668270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd like nothing more than to join in a read the Marvel stuff, but I'll stick to the movies I think. I'm one of those people who gets incredibly confused by the constant rebooting of different lines, at different times, but things all look the same (To my uneducated eyes). I also dislike the cross-over stuff; In my mind the Xmen live off in Xmen Universe and don't cross over to the Avengers or Fantastic 4. Otherwise surely Iron Man would be complaining about constant mutant riots and these massive Sentinel things etc.<br /> <br /> I did read Spawn for a while, in my youth, but lost my way with it and even that went a bit weird with different time-periods covered. I tried with Fathom, Witchblade, Darkness and again it seemed to get very convoluted quite quickly.<br /> <br /> For me the logic of jumping in on a #0 or #1 makes perfect sense ... but everything appears to reference each other? Or mention it in passing like multi-verses in some of the worlds. It all just appears needlessly and stupdily complex and confusing. I don't want to start getting into &quot;Hero #1&quot; through to issue #10 to then have it reference something two reboots ago as part of some shared continuity that I then look back at and see is some massive list of issues to read through (Purchase!) to get up to speed.<br /> <br /> At least the movies are all seperate right ... though the news of Spiderman in the Avengers confused me until I read he was always part of that team or something?<br /> <br /> I'll stick to the movies I think .... less brain-damaging!</div> </div> </div> </div>Spider-Man has been a reserve Avenger for a long time, but only joined full-time in last decade or so.<br /> <br /> And the X-men being segregated to the X-Universe is just wrong, on so may levels! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/12.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div> &quot;A free society is one in which it is safe to be unpopular.&quot;<br /> <span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\">I find <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=3&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwin4NXQzLfXAhVGySYKHV3TDXYQFgg3MAI&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.ign.com%2Farticles%2F2017%2F04%2F25%2Fmarvels-nick-spencer-on-captain-americas-betrayal-and-handling-fan-reactions&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EcsX7n5ebntygb0TizyGl\" target=\"_blank\">Nick Spencer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=4&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiK7d7szLfXAhXCWSYKHTpKBbcQFgg4MAM&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRichard_B._Spencer&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tuP6phfINia-bh_jWsDT_\" target=\"_blank\">Richard Spencer</a> to be equally repulsive.</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","name":"Mangle77","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6138_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-17T14:25:43-07:00","id":668801,"title":"","content":"The way things are looking, Marvel's going to be pushing the Inhumans as the new odd men out and culling the X-men in order to support the MCU movies and tv shows.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-17T14:41:16-07:00","id":668840,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=668801#post668801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The way things are looking, Marvel's going to be pushing the Inhumans as the new odd men out and culling the X-men in order to support the MCU movies and tv shows.</div> </div> </div> </div>At least until 20th Century Fox realizes the futility of fighting Disney for them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-17T15:17:30-07:00","id":668870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Papa Bear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/662532-can-someone-explain-superhero-comics-to-me?p=668840#post668840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At least until 20th Century Fox realizes the futility of fighting Disney for them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, they already have them. And they've been putting out some pretty good movies with the line. And Ryan Reynolds is <i>finally </i>Deadpool. The only ones really losing in this case are the comic fans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-17-2015, 06:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://for